    iast: 'madhukaiṭabhavidhvaṃsi vidhātṛvarade namaḥ |\nrūpaṃ dehi jayaṃ dehi yaśo dehi dviṣo jahi ||3||\nmahiṣāsuranirnāśi bhaktānāṃ sukhade namaḥ |\nrūpaṃ dehi jayaṃ dehi yaśo dehi dviṣo jahi ||4||\ndhūmranetravadhe devi dharmakāmārthadāyini |\nrūpaṃ dehi jayaṃ dehi yaśo dehi dviṣo jahi ||5||\nraktabījavadhe devi caṇḍamuṇḍavināśini |\nrūpaṃ dehi jayaṃ dehi yaśo dehi dviṣo jahi ||6||\nniśumbhaśumbhanirnāśi trailokyaśubhade namaḥ |\nrūpaṃ dehi jayaṃ dehi yaśo dehi dviṣo jahi ||7||',
    conceptIds: ['sadhana-samara', 'madhu-kaitabha', 'mahishasura'],
    translation: 'O Destroyer of Madhu-Kaiṭabha, giver of boons to Brahmā — give me form (beauty/vision), give victory, give fame, destroy my foes! O Annihilator of Mahiṣāsura, giver of joy to devotees... O Slayer of Dhūmranetra, giver of dharma-desire-wealth... O Slayer of Raktabīja, destroyer of Caṇḍa-Muṇḍa... O Destroyer of Śumbha-Niśumbha, auspicious to the three worlds — rūpaṃ dehi, jayaṃ dehi, yaśo dehi, dviṣo jahi!',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (Hindi: rūpaṃ dehi — 5 adhikāra-levels):**
Each demon here is previewed; its full inner meaning opens in its Carita (Madhu = sweet inertia, Mahiṣa = animal ego, Dhūmranetra = smoky perception, Raktabīja = multiplying vasanas, Śumbha = I-ness). The refrain is the core; take the meaning matching your lack (abhāvaṃ pūraya = dehi = fill the lack):
1. Rūpaṃ dehi: (i) Ma, give beautiful healthy form; (ii) let me see Your form; (iii) let me see the whole world as Your form; (iv) remove my sense of lack of beauty; (v) highest — rūpyate = what alone deserves definition, the Paramātma-vastu; let me know it.
2. Jayaṃ dehi and Yaśo dehi and Dviṣo jahi are unfolded fully in next group (Argala 8-13) with all 5 levels each — victory as satya-pratiṣṭhā (satyameva jayate), fame as “I am Your son” / white sattva / immortality (kīrtiryasya sa jīvati), foes as outer enemies → kāma-krodha → anti-sādhanā bhāvas → threefold karma-phala → all knowledge-dharma veils.
3. For beginners: ask like a straightforward child. Whatever abhāva you feel, place it before Ma. Rishis themselves asked cheerfully; asking does not block Brahman-knowledge. Ma is Kalpataru — ask with trust and you receive; if unreceived, trust was incomplete.`,
