    iast: 'na caivāpāṭavaṃ tasya bhayaṃ kvāpi na jāyate |\nnāpamṛtyuvaśaṃ yāti mṛte ca mokṣamāpnuyāt ||10||\njñātvā prārabhya kurvīta hyakurvāṇo vinaśyati |\ntato jñātvaiva sampūrṇamidaṃ prārabhyate budhaiḥ ||11||\nsaubhāgyādi ca yatkiñcid dṛśyate lalanājane |\ntatsarvaṃ tatprasādena tena jāpyamidaṃ śubham ||12||\naiśvaryaṃ yatprasādena saubhāgyārogyasampadaḥ |\nśatruhāniḥ paro mokṣaḥ stūyate sā na kiṃ janaiḥ ||13||\ncaṇḍikāṃ hṛdayenāpi smaran satataṃ naraḥ |\nhṛdi rūpaṃ cintayan devyāḥ kīlakaṃ vāraṇaṃ smaret ||14||',
    conceptIds: ['sadhana-samara', 'cit-shakti'],
    translation: 'He gets no disability, no fear anywhere, no untimely death; after death attains liberation. Knowing this, begin; neglecting, one perishes. Wise begin only after full knowing. Whatever luck is seen in women is Her grace — hence chant this. Wealth, luck-health-riches, foe-destruction, supreme mokṣa come by Her grace — why would people not praise Her? Remembering Caṇḍikā in heart, meditating Her form, recall the Keelaka-bar.',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary:**
1. **Fruit of unpinning**: Freedom from apāṭava (incapacity/disease), fear, apamṛtyu (untimely fall), plus videha-mukti. This is adhikāra-phala: a mind made Mother-facing becomes fear-proof because it no longer identifies with fragile body.
2. **Jñātvā prārabhya**: Ritual without knowing the pin perishes (vinaśyati). Wise (budhaiḥ) first complete knowing, then begin. For beginners: study Argala-Kīlaka meaning once, then do Saptaśatī; otherwise recitation stays mechanical.
3. **Why women mentioned?** Saubhāgya seen in women is visible prasāda of Mother-power (strī = śakti). Honouring it reminds that grace, not ego-effort, gives aiśvarya-ārogya-śatruhāni-mokṣa. Hence “why would people not praise?” Keep Caṇḍikā in heart, Her form in heart, and the pin-bar (vāraṇa) ever in mind.`,
