    devanagari: 'महामायानुभावेन यथा मन्वन्तराधिपः।\nस बभूव महाभागः सावर्णिस्तनयो रवेः॥',
    iast: 'mahāmāyānubhāvena yathā manvantarādhipaḥ |\nsa babhūva mahābhāgaḥ sāvarṇistanayo raveḥ ||',
    conceptIds: ['mahamaya', 'cit-shakti', 'sadhana-samara'],
    translation: 'Hear how that greatly fortunate Sāvarṇi, son of Sūrya (Ravi), became the lord of the Manvantara (the sovereign of an entire cosmic age of humanity) solely by the gracious will-power (anubhāva) of Mahāmāyā.',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (Hindi source: Mahāmāyānubhāvena):**
1. **What is Anubhāva? (anu + bhāva)**: Mahāmāyā is pure conscious power (cinmayī śakti), hence hard to grasp directly (durvijñeyā). But She appears every moment immediately-after (anu) as feeling-thought (bhāva). Every ripple in your heart — lust or anger, taste of sweet or sound, kindness or patience — is Her anubhāva. Bhāva condensed becomes this solid world. So long as She is bhāva She is grasped only by mind; when that bhāva thickens (ghanībhūta) it is grasped by outer senses. Bhāva is Ma Herself. The world is nothing but bhāva.
2. **Proof that bhāva is inside (svagata-bhāva)**: One young woman enters a room. The husband feels wife-bhāva, his father feels daughter-in-law-bhāva, the son feels mother-bhāva, the servant feels mistress-bhāva, a lustful friend feels desire-bhāva, and a chained tiger feels food-bhāva. Were so many bhāvas inside that one form? No — each was your own inner bhāva. So too a thorn prick: was pain in the thorn or in you? Mango sweetness: in the mango or your experience? All world-enjoyment is bhāva resting inside you. The supreme seat (paramapada) is Mahāmāyā; its meanings welling up one-by-one inside you are Her anubhāva.
3. **Practice instruction for restless mind**: When you sit for Ma and monkey-thoughts, money, fame, lust crowd in, do not despair saying “I am unfit, sādhanā is only for forest saints.” That restlessness itself is Ma coming in disguise (chadmaveśī). Do not try to violently push bhāva away. Honour each bhāva as Ma, bow to it, do satya-pratiṣṭhā (“this too is Ma”). Stillness (citta-sthairya) is not the precondition of sādhanā; it is the result. By hathayoga or forced breath-holding you get only dull sleep (jaḍa-samādhi), not prajñā. When Ma comes, mind stills by itself as darkness flees at sunrise. Hence restlessness is Ma’s blessing (āśīrvāda) — it proves life is still moving toward Her.
4. **Mahābhāga, Aṣṭama, Manvantarādhipa**: He who reaches Manutva (the collective human consciousness, from root man = to know; vyashti manuja ripens into samashti Manu) becomes aṣṭama — master of 8 siddhis (aṇimā, laghimā etc.) and freed from 8 bonds (pāśas: hatred, shame, fear, blame etc.), attaining God-likeness (bhagavat-sārūpya). Established in universal Manu-consciousness he becomes lord of the Manvantara: he can directly perceive the samskāra-heaps of every human, past births, merit and suffering, and by will-power turn their downward pull upward. All this comes only by Mahāmāyā’s favourable will (anukūlā icchā, kṛpā), never by mere logic. Even unwillingly, once caught in Her attraction, matṛ-mukhī gati begins and ignorance thins daily.`,
    keyPoints: [
      '**Anubhāva = Every Feeling is Ma**: Kama, krodha, daya, hunger, joy — all are Mahamaya appearing moment-by-moment as bhava.',
      '**Svagata-bhava Proof**: One woman evokes six different bhavas in six seers; pain and sweetness live inside you, not in objects.',
      '**Restlessness is Blessing**: Do not fight thoughts; honour each as disguised Ma. Stillness follows Ma, forced suppression gives only dull sleep.',
      '**Aṣṭama and Manvantarādhipa**: Manutva gives 8 powers, freedom from 8 bonds, and direct perception of humanity’s collective mind — solely by Ma’s grace.'
    ]
