    devanagari: 'ततः स्वपुरमायातो निजदेशाधिपोऽभवत्।\nआक्रान्तः स महाभागस्तैस्तदा प्रबलारिभिः॥\nअमात्यैर्बलिभिर्दुष्टैर्दुर्बलस्य दुरात्मभिः।\nकोशो बलं चापहृतं तत्रापि स्वपुरे ततः॥',
    iast: 'tataḥ svapuramāyāto nijadeśādhipo\'bhavat |\nākrāntaḥ sa mahābhāgastairtadā prabalāribhiḥ ||\namātyairbalibhirdusṭairdurbalasya durātmabhiḥ |\nkośo balaṃ cāpahṛtaṃ tatrāpi svapure tataḥ ||',
    conceptIds: ['suratha-samadhi', 'sadhana-samara', 'mahamaya'],
    translation: 'Then defeated, the greatly fortunate Suratha returned to his own city and remained merely as lord of his palace precincts, overpowered by mighty foes. There, even in his own city, his treasury (kośa) and army (bala) were seized by his own powerful, wicked, evil-natured ministers.',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (Hindi source 1.6-7: svapura + amātyas):**
1. **Svapura = the heart-cave**: After defeat in the bhāva-battle (outer samskaras), the jīva dives into forgetfulness and enters svapura — the hṛdaya-guhā, the primeval supreme seat (purāṇa parama-pada), seeking eternal peace. Even there he finds no peace: powerful hostile ministers wait inside.
2. **Who are the ministers? Dharma-samskaras**: The very Vedic injunctions (vidhi-niṣedha) and karmakāṇḍa rituals — japa, pūjā, vrata, tapas, strict rules (red flower for Viṣṇu, bel-leaf for Śiva, breath rules, posture rules) — which once helped like wise counsellors to build dharma-rājya and white sattvic fame, now become the final, strongest enemy. As thorn removes thorn, scriptural duty lifts you from downfall and awakens hunger for Ma; but Ma is beyond dharma, beyond adharma, beyond karma — unspeakable non-dual bliss. First dharma-rājya, then ātma-rājya. First dharma, then Ma. Dharma is the ladder (sopāna); the jīva who is ever-free cannot live forever in a golden chain. To attain sva (Self) he must one day become sarvasvānta (stripped of all) and take shelter in svapura. Gītā’s voice rings: “īśvaraḥ sarvabhūtānāṃ hṛddeśe — take refuge in Him alone with all being.”
3. **Why three adjectives — balibhiḥ, duṣṭaiḥ, durātmabhiḥ?** Balibhiḥ (mighty): dharma-habit is harder to drop than adharma-habit. A drunkard drops drink faster than a devout brāhmaṇa drops his thrice-daily sandhyā. Duṣṭaiḥ (corrupt): when “kṣīṇe puṇye martyalokaṃ viśanti (when merit ends, you fall back to mortal world)” truly echoes in the heart, can you still call merit-making innocent? Durātmabhiḥ (evil-natured, sticky): you know dharma is not ātma-rājya, not mokṣa, not Ma’s lap — yet you cannot leave it; it will not leave you; it blocks svapura’s door.
4. **What was looted? Kośa and bala**: Kośa here is ānandamaya-kośa — Ma’s golden temple, the eternal rest-house (śāntiniketan, Goloka, nitya-rāsamaṇḍala of Vaiṣṇavas). Sādhanā is withdrawing self-sense from annamaya (body) through prāṇa-mana-vijñāna up into ānandamaya (“I am eternal bliss-consciousness”). Ritual samskaras loot that, giving only fragmentary pleasure. Bala looted is your true power — nitya-śuddha-buddha-mukta (ever-pure-awake-free) — ministers make you feel ever-impure-bound. Patañjali agrees: “sthūlā vṛttayaḥ ordinary foes, sūkṣmāḥ mahā-pratipakṣāḥ” — gross lust-anger harms less than subtle dharma-conceit. Hence Rāmprasād sings: “tie the two goats dharma-adharma to a contemptible post; if mind objects, sacrifice them with the sword of knowledge.”
5. **Culminating viṣāda-yoga**: Gītā’s Kurukṣetra grief was in manomaya (outer signs: Gāṇḍīva slipping, mouth drying). Suratha’s grief in svapura is in vijñānamaya-ānandamaya — higher adhikāra, higher grief, invisible outside. Worldly griefs (sick man, bereaved mother, widowed child, starving skeleton) are nothing beside the anupamya (incomparable) viraha of the ānandamaya climber — “as Brahman is indescribable, so is His separation.” Only a Rādhā (ārādhikā) knows Kṛṣṇa-viraha.`,
    keyPoints: [
      '**Svapura = Heart-Cave**: After outer defeat the seeker turns inward, only to meet subtler foes inside.',
      '**Dharma-samskara = Strongest Enemy**: Ritual merit (balibhiḥ, duṣṭaiḥ, durātmabhiḥ) is stickier than sin; thorn removes thorn, then both must be dropped.',
      '**Looted Ānandamaya-kośa**: The golden rest-house of bliss is the true treasure; sadhana is carrying self-sense up to it.',
      '**Higher Grief**: Suratha’s silent viṣāda in vijñānamaya surpasses Arjuna’s visible grief in manomaya.'
    ]
