    iast: 'jñānināmapi cetāṃsi devī bhagavatī hi sā |\nbalādākṛṣya mohāya mahāmāyā prayacchati ||\ntayā visṛjyate viśvaṃ jagadetaccarācaram |\nsaiṣā prasannā varadā nṛṇāṃ bhavati muktaye ||',
    conceptIds: ['mahamaya', 'cit-shakti'],
    translation: 'Sage Medhas replied: "She, the blessed Goddess Mahāmāyā, forcibly draws even the minds of the wise and casts them into delusion! By Her this entire moving and unmoving universe is projected. And She alone, when propitiated with devotion, becomes the bestower of boons and grants supreme liberation!"',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary:**
This is the foundational aphorism of the Śākta worldview. *Mahāmāyā* is not an accidental blemish, nor is She inert illusion (*Mithyā* in the sense of an absolute void). She is *Bhagavatī*—the conscious sovereign power (*Cit-Śakti*) of the Supreme Divine. 
Her power operates in two modes:
1. *Avidyā-Rūpa* (The Veiling Power): Forcibly drawing even contemplative minds outward into the cosmic drama (*Balād-ākṛṣya*).
2. *Vidyā-Rūpa* (The Unveiling Grace): When worshipped not as external world-matter but as the Mother-Consciousness, She turns Her gaze (*Prasannā*) and dissolves all bondages, granting *Mukti*. 
Hence, the spiritual aspirant must not combat Nature as an enemy, but propitiate Her as the affectionate Divine Mother who holds the keys to liberation.`,
