    section: 'Madhyama Carita (Viṣṇu-Granthi Bheda / Mahiṣāsura Vadha)',
    devanagari: 'ददावशून्यं सुरया पानपात्रं धनाधिपः ।\nशेषश्च सर्वनागेशो महामणिविभूषितम् ॥२९॥\nनागहारं ददौ तस्यै धत्ते यः पृथिवीमिमाम् ।\nअन्यैरपि सुरैर्देवी भूषणैरायुधैस्तथा ॥३०॥\nसम्मानिता ननादोच्चैः साट्टहासं मुहुर्मुहुः ।\nतस्या नादेन घोरेण कृत्स्नमापूरितं नभः ॥३१॥\nअमायतातिमहता प्रतिशब्दो महानभूत् ।\nचुक्षुभुः सकला लोकाः समुद्राश्च चकम्पिरे ॥३२॥\nचचाल वसुधा चेलुः सकलाश्च महीधराः ।\nजयेति देवाश्च मुदा तामूचुः सिंहवाहिनीम् ॥३३॥',
    iast: 'dadāvaśūnyaṃ surayā pānapātraṃ dhanādhipaḥ |\nśeṣaśca sarvanāgeśo mahāmaṇivibhūṣitam ||29||\nnāgahāraṃ dadau tasyai dhatte yaḥ pṛthivīmimām |\nanyairapi surairdevī bhūṣaṇairāyudhaistathā ||30||\nsammānitā nanādoccaiḥ sāṭṭahāsaṃ muhurmuhuḥ |\ntasyā nādena ghoreṇa kṛtsnamāpūritaṃ nabhaḥ ||31||\namāyatātimahatā pratiśabdo mahānabhūt |\ncukṣubhuḥ sakalā lokāḥ samudrāśca cakampire ||32||\ncacāla vasudhā celuḥ sakalāśca mahīdharāḥ |\njayeti devāśca mudā tāmūcuḥ siṃhavāhinīm ||33||',
    conceptIds: ['mahishasura', 'cit-shakti', 'sadhana-samara'],
    translation: 'Kubera (lord of wealth) gave a wine-filled drinking cup; Śeṣa, lord of all serpents who bears this earth, gave a serpent-necklace gleaming with a great jewel. Honoured thus by all the other gods with jewels and weapons, Devī uttered a mighty war-roar again and again; with Her dread sound the whole sky was filled. A boundless, exceeding-great echo answered; all worlds trembled, oceans shook, earth swayed and mountains rocked — and the gods cried joyfully to the lion-rider: “Victory!”',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (the arming completed, the roar):**
1. **Wine-cup and serpent-jewel**: Kubera’s cup (madirā-pātra) is bliss-inebriation (ānanda-mada) — preview of the madhu-pāna by which She will slay Mahiṣa (dm-3-38). Śeṣa’s jewel is kuṇḍalinī, the earth-bearing remnant-consciousness at the spine’s root, now offered to the Whole. Every god gives its best (sva-sva): the practitioner too must surrender signature strengths, not leftovers.
2. **Echo (pratiśabda)**: Her roar fills space (ākāśa = inner space cleared); the echo answering is conscience awakening — the heart now reverberates what wisdom uttered. Worlds trembling, oceans shaking, mountains rocking = the three kośa-layers (body, prāṇa, mind) all stirred at once; no sheath left unstirred.
3. **“Jaya!”**: The gods’ victory-cry is the faculties’ first confidence after long defeat — celebrate intermediate wins in sādhanā; joy itself is armour. Next (dm-2-34): Mahiṣa mobilises his armies.`,
