    section: 'Madhyama Carita (Viṣṇu-Granthi Bheda / Mahiṣāsura Vadha)',
    devanagari: 'युध्यमानौ ततस्तौ तु तस्मान्नागान्महीं गतौ ।\nयुयुधातेऽतिसंरब्धौ प्रहारैरतिदारुणैः ॥१४॥\nततो वेगात्खमुत्पत्य निपत्य च मृगारिणा ।\nकरप्रहारेण शिरश्चामरस्य पृथक्कृतम् ॥१५॥\nउदग्रश्च रणे देव्या शिलावृक्षादिभिर्हतः ।\nदन्तमुष्टितलैश्चैव करालश्च निपातितः ॥१६॥\nदेवी क्रुद्धा गदापातैश्चूर्णयामास चोद्धतम् ।\nबाष्कलं भिन्दिपालेन बाणैस्ताम्रं तथान्धकम् ॥१७॥\nउग्रास्यमुग्रवीर्यं च तथैव च महाहनुम् ।\nत्रिनेत्रा च त्रिशूलेन जघान परमेश्वरी ॥१८॥\nबिडालस्यासिना कायात्पातयामास वै शिरः ।\nदुर्धरं दुर्मुखं चोभौ शरैर्निन्ये यमक्षयम् ॥१९॥\nएवं सङ्क्षीयमाणे तु स्वसैन्ये महिषासुरः ।\nमाहिषेण स्वरूपेण त्रासयामास तान् गणान् ॥२०॥\nकांश्चित्तुण्डप्रहारेण खुरक्षेपैस्तथापरान् ।\nलाङ्गलताडितांश्चान्यान्शृङ्गाभ्यां च विदारितान् ॥२१॥\nवेगेन कांश्चिदपरान्नादेन भ्रमणेन च ।\nनिःश्वासपवनेनान्यान्यातयामास भूतले ॥२२॥\nनिपात्य प्रमथानीकमभ्यधावत सोऽसुरः ।\nसिंहं हन्तुं महादेव्याः कोपं चक्रे ततोऽम्बिका ॥२३॥\nसोऽपि कोपान्महावीर्यः खुरक्षुण्णमहीतलः ।\nशृङ्गाभ्यां पर्वतानुच्चांश्चिक्षेप च ननाद च ॥२४॥\nवेगभ्रमणविक्षुण्णा मही तस्य व्यशीर्यत ।\nलाङ्गलेनाहतश्चाब्धिः प्लावयामास सर्वतः ॥२५॥\nधुतशृङ्गविभिन्नाश्च खण्डं खण्ड ययुर्घनाः ।\nश्वासानिलास्ताः शतशो निपेतुर्नभसोऽचलाः ॥२६॥\nइति क्रोधसमाध्मातमापतन्तं महासुरम् ।\nदृष्ट्वा सा चण्डिका कोपं तद्वधाय तदाकरोत् ॥२७॥',
    iast: 'yudhyamānau tatastau tu tasmānnāgānmahīṃ gatau |\nyuyudhāte\'tisaṃrabdhau prahārairatidāruṇaiḥ ||14||\ntato vegātkhamutpatya nipatya ca mṛgāriṇā |\nkaraprahāreṇa śiraścāmarasya pṛthakkṛtam ||15||\nudagraśca raṇe devyā śilāvṛkṣādibhirhataḥ |\ndantamuṣṭitalaiścaiva karālaśca nipātitaḥ ||16||\ndevī kruddhā gadāpātaiścūrṇayāmāsa coddhatam |\nbāṣkalaṃ bhindipālena bāṇaistāmraṃ tathāndhakam ||17||\nugrāsyamugravīryaṃ ca tathaiva ca mahāhanum |\ntrinetrā ca triśūlena jaghāna parameśvarī ||18||\nbiḍālasyāsinā kāyātpātayāmāsa vai śiraḥ |\ndurdharaṃ durmukhaṃ cobhau śarairninye yamakṣayam ||19||\nevaṃ saṅkṣīyamāṇe tu svasainye mahiṣāsuraḥ |\nmāhiṣeṇa svarūpeṇa trāsayāmāsa tān gaṇān ||20||\nkāṃścittuṇḍaprahāreṇa khurakṣepaistathāparān |\nlāṅgalatāḍitāṃścānyānśṛṅgābhyāṃ ca vidāritān ||21||\nvegena kāṃścidaparānnādena bhramaṇena ca |\nniḥśvāsapavanenānyānyātayāmāsa bhūtale ||22||\nnipātya pramathānīkamabhyadhāvata so\'suraḥ |\nsiṃhaṃ hantuṃ mahādevyāḥ kopaṃ cakre tato\'mbikā ||23||\nso\'pi kopānmahāvīryaḥ khurakṣuṇṇamahītalaḥ |\nśṛṅgābhyāṃ parvatānuccāṃścikṣepa ca nanāda ca ||24||\nvegabhramaṇavikṣuṇṇā mahī tasya vyaśīryata |\nlāṅgalenāhataścābdhiḥ plāvayāmāsa sarvataḥ ||25||\ndhutaśṛṅgavibhinnāśca khaṇḍaṃ khaṇḍa yayurghanāḥ |\nśvāsānilāstāḥ śataśo nipeturnabhaso\'calāḥ ||26||\niti krodhasamādhmātamāpatantaṃ mahāsuram |\ndṛṣṭvā sā caṇḍikā kopaṃ tadvadhāya tadākarot ||27||',
    conceptIds: ['mahishasura', 'sadhana-samara', 'cit-shakti'],
    translation: 'Fighting on, the two descended from the elephant and fought furiously with dread blows. Then springing into the sky and swooping, the lion (mṛgāri) severed Cāmara’s head with a paw-stroke. Udagra fell to rocks and trees hurled by Devī; Karāla to teeth, fists and slaps. Wrathful Devī pounded Uddhata with mace-blows, Bāṣkala with the javelin, Tāmra and Andhaka with arrows; the three-eyed supreme Goddess slew Ugrāsya, Ugravīrya and Mahāhanu with the trident; with the sword She took Biḍāla’s head from his trunk; Durdhara and Durmukha both She sent to Death’s abode with shafts. As his army thus dwindled, Mahiṣāsura in buffalo-form terrorised the gaṇas — some with muzzle-blows, others with hoof-kicks, some lashed with his tail, others ripped with horns; some felled by his rush, his roar, his circling; others tormented to the ground by his breath-wind. Felling the pramatha-host, the demon charged to kill great Devī’s lion — then Ambikā grew wrathful. He too in rage, churning earth with hooves, tossed high mountains with his horns and bellowed; earth, pounded by his rushing circles, cracked; the ocean, struck by his tail, flooded all sides; clouds, split by shaking horns, flew in shreds; hundredfold breath-winds felled mountains from the sky. Seeing that great demon rushing, swollen with rage, Caṇḍikā then grew wrathful to slay him.',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (the generals fall; the buffalo rampages):**
1. **The muster-roll of excuses**: Udagra (the Upstart), Karāla (the Gruesome), Uddhata (the Arrogant), Bāṣkala, Tāmra-Andhaka (the Dark Pair), Ugrāsya-Ugravīrya-Mahāhanu (Fierce-face, Fierce-might, Great-jaw), Biḍāla (the Cat — stealthy lapses), Durdhara-Durmukha (the Unbearable and Foul-mouthed) — each excuse-network needs its fitting weapon: rocks-trees (plain facts), teeth-fists (close disgust), mace (heavy resolve), javelin (hurled decisions), arrows (precise insights), trident (threefold balance), sword (cutting viveka). Beginners: name your excuses; named demons are half-slain.
2. **Buffalo = brute tamas unleashed**: Muzzle (taste), hooves (habit-tracks), tail (lingering traces), horns (dualistic thrusts), rush-roar-circling (restlessness), breath-wind (stormy prāṇa) — the full artillery of inertia when cornered. It tosses mountains (stable resolves), floods the ocean (subconscious deluge), shreds clouds (obscures higher mind), drops peaks from the sky (ideals crash). When sādhanā corners tamas, expect a storm before stillness.
3. **Her wrath answers his**: “Dṛṣṭvā sā caṇḍikā kopaṃ tadvadhāya tadākarot” — divine kopa is not hatred but concentrated resolve answering scattered rage. The duel proper begins (dm-3-28): noose, shapeshifting, madhu-pāna.`,
