    devanagari: 'ऋषिरुवाच ।\nशक्रादयः सुरगणा निहतेऽतिवीर्ये तस्मिन्दुरात्मनि सुरारिबले च देव्या ।\nतां तुष्टुवुः प्रणतिनम्रशिरोधरांसा वाग्भिः प्रहर्षपुलकोद्गमचारुदेहाः ॥१॥\nदेव्या यया ततमिदं जगदात्मशक्त्या निःशेषदेवगणशक्तिसमूहमूर्त्या ।\nतामम्बिकामखिलदेवमहर्षिपूज्यां भक्त्या नताः स्म विदधातु शुभानि सा नः ॥२॥',
    iast: 'ṛṣiruvāca |\nśakrādayaḥ suragaṇā nihate\'tivīrye tasmindurātmani surāribale ca devyā |\ntāṃ tuṣṭuvuḥ praṇatinamraśirodharāṃsā vāgbhiḥ praharṣapulakodgamacārudehāḥ ||1||\ndevyā yayā tatamidaṃ jagadātmaśaktyā niḥśeṣadevagaṇaśaktisamūhamūrtyā |\ntāmambikāmakhiladevamaharṣipūjyāṃ bhaktyā natāḥ sma vidadhātu śubhāni sā naḥ ||2||',
    conceptIds: ['cit-shakti', 'mahishasura', 'sadhana-samara'],
    translation: 'The Ṛṣi said: When that exceeding-valiant evil soul and the demon host were slain by Devī, Indra and the gods praised Her — necks and shoulders bowed in homage, bodies lovely with joy-born horripilation: “She by whom, through Her own power, this world is spread — She whose body is the combined might of all the gods’ hosts — to that Ambikā, worshipped by all gods and great seers, we bow in devotion. May She grant us weal.”',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (Śakrādi-stuti opens; thanksgiving first):**
1. **Praise before philosophy**: Victory’s first act is gratitude, not analysis. Necks-shoulders bowed (praṇati), bodies thrilled (pulaka) — thanksgiving is bodily, not merely verbal. For beginners: after every inner win (a temptation refused, a week of steady practice), pause and praise; unacknowledged grace leaks away.
2. **Verse 2 = the stuti’s thesis**: the world (jagat) is spread (tata) by Her own power (ātmaśaktyā); Her body (mūrti) is the combined śakti of all gods. This is pure non-dual Śākta metaphysics in one breath — creation is not built by Her but IS Her body. Guptavatī and Nāgojī both stress: She was already śakti before the gods lent their tejas; the lending only revealed Her.
3. **“May She grant us weal” (śubhāni)**: the gods ask nothing crude — only śubha (auspiciousness). The stuti that follows (dm-4-3 onward) unpacks Her as Śrī, Buddhi, Śraddhā, Smṛti, Night, Hunger, Shadow, Power — every life-function transfigured.`,
