    conceptIds: ['cit-shakti', 'sadhana-samara'],
    translation: '“Guard us with the spear, Devī; guard us with the sword, Ambikā; guard us with the bell’s clang and the bowstring’s twang. Guard us in the east and west, Caṇḍikā; guard the south; by whirling Thy spear guard the north, O Īśvarī. Whatever gentle forms of Thine roam the triple world, and whatever exceeding-terrible — with them guard us and the earth. Whatever weapons Thine, Ambikā — sword, spear, mace and the rest that dwell in Thy sprout-like hands — with them guard us on every side.”',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (stuti becomes kavaca):**
1. **Praise turns to armour**: the stuti’s crown is a protection-prayer — after metaphysics comes shelter. Spear (one-pointedness), sword (viveka), bell (alertness that scatters sleep), bowstring-twang (mind-string taut, humming readiness) — four sound-weapons guard the four directions; whirling spear (bhrāmaṇa — revolving awareness) covers the north (the direction of Kubera-wealth and of the pole-star goal).
2. **Gentle and terrible both guard**: saumyāni (mild forms — Lakṣmī’s smile, lullabies) and atyartha-ghorāṇi (exceeding-dread — Kālī’s fangs, cremation-fire) are equally “Thine” and equally invoked. For beginners: do not edit the Mother — invite Her dread forms too; fear of Her fierceness is itself the last impurity. “Guard us and the earth” (bhuvam) — protection extends from self to world.
3. **Weapons that dwell in sprout-hands**: karapallava (sprout-like, tender hands) holding sword-spear-mace — tenderness wields fierceness; this is the whole Kavaca doctrine (dm-kavacha-3) condensed into one verse. Recite this quartet morning-evening as a portable armour.`,
