    devanagari: 'एवं स्तवादियुक्तानां देवानां तत्र पार्वती ।\nस्नातुमभ्याययौ तोये जाह्नव्या नृपनन्दन ॥३७॥\nसाब्रवीत्तान्सुरान्सुभ्रूर्भवद्भिः स्तूयतेऽत्र का ।\nशरीरकोशतश्चास्याः समुद्भूताब्रवीच्छिवा ॥३८॥\nस्तोत्रं ममैतत्क्रियते शुम्भदैत्यनिराकृतैः ।\nदेवैः समेतैः समरे निशुम्भेन पराजितैः ॥३९॥\nशरीरकोशाद्यत्तस्याः पार्वत्या निःसृताम्बिका ।\nकौशिकीति समस्तेषु ततो लोकेषु गीयते ॥४०॥\nतस्यां विनिर्गतायां तु कृष्णाभूत्सापि पार्वती ।\nकालिकेति समाख्याता हिमाचलकृताश्रया ॥४१॥\nततोऽम्बिकां परं रूपं बिभ्राणां सुमनोहरम् ।\nददर्श चण्डो मुण्डश्च भृत्यौ शुम्भनिशुम्भयोः ॥४२॥\nताभ्यां शुम्भाय चाख्याता सातीव सुमनोहरा ।\nकाप्यास्ते स्त्री महाराज भासयन्ती हिमाचलम् ॥४३॥\nनैव तादृक् क्वचिद्रूपं दृष्टं केनचिदुत्तमम् ।\nज्ञायतां काप्यसौ देवी गृह्यतां चासुरेश्वर ॥४४॥\nस्त्रीरत्नमतिचार्वङ्गी द्योतयन्ती दिशस्त्विषा ।\nसा तु तिष्ठति दैत्येन्द्र तां भवान्द्रष्टुमर्हति ॥४५॥\nयानि रत्नानि मणयो गजाश्वादीनि वै प्रभो ।\nत्रैलोक्ये तु समस्तानि साम्प्रतं भान्ति ते गृहे ॥४६॥',
    iast: 'evaṃ stavādiyuktānāṃ devānāṃ tatra pārvatī |\nsnātumabhyāyayau toye jāhnavyā nṛpanandana ||37||\nsābravīttānsurānsubhrūrbhavadbhiḥ stūyate\'tra kā |\nśarīrakośataścāsyāḥ samudbhūtābravīcchivā ||38||\nstotraṃ mamaitatkriyate śumbhadaityanirākṛtaiḥ |\ndevaiḥ sametaiḥ samare niśumbhena parājitaiḥ ||39||\nśarīrakośādyattasyāḥ pārvatyā niḥsṛtāmbikā |\nkauśikīti samasteṣu tato lokeṣu gīyate ||40||\ntasyāṃ vinirgatāyāṃ tu kṛṣṇābhūtsāpi pārvatī |\nkāliketi samākhyātā himācalakṛtāśrayā ||41||\ntato\'mbikāṃ paraṃ rūpaṃ bibhrāṇāṃ sumanoharam |\ndadarśa caṇḍo muṇḍaśca bhṛtyau śumbhaniśumbhayoḥ ||42||\ntābhyāṃ śumbhāya cākhyātā sātīva sumanoharā |\nkāpyāste strī mahārāja bhāsayantī himācalam ||43||\nnaiva tādṛk kvacidrūpaṃ dṛṣṭaṃ kenaciduttamam |\njñāyatāṃ kāpyasau devī gṛhyatāṃ cāsureśvara ||44||\nstrīratnamaticārvaṅgī dyotayantī diśastviṣā |\nsā tu tiṣṭhati daityendra tāṃ bhavāndraṣṭumarhati ||45||\nyāni ratnāni maṇayo gajāśvādīni vai prabho |\ntrailokye tu samastāni sāmprataṃ bhānti te gṛhe ||46||',
    conceptIds: ['shumbha-nishumbha', 'cit-shakti', 'sadhana-samara'],
    translation: 'While the gods thus praised with hymns, Pārvatī came there to bathe in Jāhnavī’s waters, O prince. Fair-browed, She asked those gods: “Whom do you praise here?” — and from Her body-sheath emerged Śivā, who said: “This hymn of mine is sung by the gods, repelled by demon Śumbha and routed in battle by Niśumbha, all assembled.” From Pārvatī’s body-sheath issued Ambikā — thence sung in all worlds as Kauśikī. And when She had come forth, Pārvatī Herself turned black — famed as Kālikā, dwelling in Himavat. Then Caṇḍa and Muṇḍa, servants of Śumbha-Niśumbha, beheld Ambikā wearing supreme, most lovely form. By them it was told to Śumbha: “A certain woman, great king, most lovely, lighting up Himavat — never was such beauty seen by any anywhere. Find out who this Goddess is; seize Her, O demon-lord. A jewel among women, exquisite-limbed, kindling the quarters with Her glow — She stands, O chief; Thou shouldst behold Her. Whatever jewels, gems, elephants, horses in the triple world — all now shine, O lord, in Thy house.”',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (Kauśikī from the sheath; beauty seen as booty):**
1. **Subtlest emergence**: Madhu-Kaiṭabha and Mahiṣa needed pooled tejas; Kauśikī issues from Gaurī’s own body-sheath (śarīra-kośa) — Rudra-granthi work is self-sheath work, consciousness differentiating itself. Pārvatī (the fair meditative form) darkens into Kālikā the moment Śakti goes forth: manifestation’s price is the source turning to night — the dark ground (Himavat-dwelling depth) holding what light expends. Nāgojī: kośa = the five sheaths; Kauśikī = their conscious core stepping out.
2. **“Whom do you praise?”**: Pārvatī asks, and Her own sheath answers — the questioner and answer are one. For beginners: when praise ripens, the praised form steps out of the praiser. Jāhnavī-bath (Ganges waters = the flowing current of purification) is the occasion: embodiment follows cleansing.
3. **Beauty seen as booty**: Caṇḍa-Muṇḍa see only rūpa (form) — ego perceives Śakti as object: “gṛhyatām” (seize Her!), “strī-ratnam” (woman-jewel for the collection). Medhas pointedly retells their sales-pitch whole: hear how temptation talks — inventory, flattery, urgency. Śumbha’s fatal error is already committed here: treating Cit as bhoga-vastu (enjoyment-object). The jewel-catalogue (dm-5-47) and embassy (dm-5-57) follow.`,
