    section: 'Uttama Carita (Rudra-Granthi Bheda / Śumbha-Niśumbha Vadha)',
    devanagari: 'ऋषिरुवाच ।\nइत्याकर्ण्य वचो देव्याः स दूतोऽमर्षपूरितः ।\nसमाचष्ट समागम्य दैत्यराजाय विस्तरात् ॥१॥\nतस्य दूतस्य तद्वाक्यमाकर्ण्य सुरराट् ततः ।\nसक्रोधः प्राह दैत्यानामधिपं धूम्रलोचनम् ॥२॥\nहे धूम्रलोचनाशु त्वं स्वसैन्यपरिवारितः ।\nतामानय बलाद्दुष्टां केशाकर्षणविह्वलाम् ॥३॥\nतत्परित्राणदः कश्चिद्यदि वोत्तिष्ठते परः ।\nस हन्तव्योऽमरो वापि यक्षो गन्धर्व एव वा ॥४॥\nतेनाज्ञप्तस्ततः शीघ्रं स दैत्यो धूम्रलोचनः ।\nवृतः षष्ट्या सहस्त्राणामसुराणां द्रुतं ययौ ॥५॥\nस दृष्ट्वा तां ततो देवीं तुहिनाचलसंस्थिताम् ।\nजगादोच्चैः प्रयाहीति मूलं शुम्भनिशुम्भयोः ॥६॥\nन चेत्प्रीत्याद्य भवती मद्भर्तारमुपैष्यति ।\nततो बलान्नयाम्यद्य केशाकर्षणविह्वलाम् ॥७॥\nदैत्येश्वरेण प्रहितो बलवान्बलसंवृतः ।\nबलान्नयसि मामेवं ततः किं ते करोम्यहम् ॥८॥\nइत्युक्तः सोऽभ्यधावत्तामसुरो धूम्रलोचनः ।\nहुङ्कारेणैव तं भस्म सा चकाराम्बिका ततः ॥९॥',
    iast: 'ṛṣiruvāca |\nityākarṇya vaco devyāḥ sa dūto\'marṣapūritaḥ |\nsamācaṣṭa samāgamya daityarājāya vistarāt ||1||\ntasya dūtasya tadvākyamākarṇya surarāṭ tataḥ |\nsakrodhaḥ prāha daityānāmadhipaṃ dhūmralocanam ||2||\nhe dhūmralocanāśu tvaṃ svasainyaparivāritaḥ |\ntāmānaya balādduṣṭāṃ keśākarṣaṇavihvalām ||3||\ntatparitrāṇadaḥ kaścidyadi vottiṣṭhate paraḥ |\nsa hantavyo\'maro vāpi yakṣo gandharva eva vā ||4||\ntenājñaptastataḥ śīghraṃ sa daityo dhūmralocanaḥ |\nvṛtaḥ ṣaṣṭyā sahastrāṇāmasurāṇāṃ drutaṃ yayau ||5||\nsa dṛṣṭvā tāṃ tato devīṃ tuhinācalasaṃsthitām |\njagādoccaiḥ prayāhīti mūlaṃ śumbhaniśumbhayoḥ ||6||\nna cetprītyādya bhavatī madbhartāramupaiṣyati |\ntato balānnayāmyadya keśākarṣaṇavihvalām ||7||\ndaityeśvareṇa prahito balavānbalasaṃvṛtaḥ |\nbalānnayasi māmevaṃ tataḥ kiṃ te karomyaham ||8||\nityuktaḥ so\'bhyadhāvattāmasuro dhūmralocanaḥ |\nhuṅkāreṇaiva taṃ bhasma sā cakārāmbikā tataḥ ||9||',
    conceptIds: ['shumbha-nishumbha', 'sadhana-samara'],
    translation: 'The Ṛṣi said: Hearing Devī’s words, the envoy, filled with indignation, returned and reported everything at length to the demon-king. Hearing the envoy’s words, the demon-sovereign in rage then addressed Dhūmralocana, lord of demons: “O Smoke-eyed, quickly, girt with thine army — fetch Her by force, the wicked one, dishevelled by hair-dragging. And if any other rescuer of Hers rises up — god, yakṣa or gandharva — he must be slain.” So commanded, the demon Dhūmralocana swiftly marched, ringed with sixty thousand demons. Seeing Her then, Devī seated on the snowy peak, he cried aloud: “Go to Śumbha-Niśumbha’s root (submit to their house)! And if out of love today, lady, thou wilt not come to my master — then I shall drag thee by force today, dishevelled by the hair.” “Sent by the demon-lord, strong, girt with strength — if thou draggest Me thus by force, what shall I do to thee?” So addressed, the demon Dhūmralocana rushed at Her — and with a mere HUṀ-kāra Ambikā then turned him to ash.',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (smoke-eyed perception ashed by syllable):**
1. **Dhūmralocana = smoky seeing**: dhūmra (smoke) + locana (eye) — perception clouded by fumes of desire and resentment. He “sees” Devī on the snow-peak (tuhinācala — cool, pure height of detachment) yet perceives only an object to drag. Sixty thousand demons = the countless rationalisations escorting one deluded perception. For beginners: notice when your “seeing” is smoking — aggrieved, hurried, grasping.
2. **Crude orders, polished cruelty**: “drag by the hair” (keśākarṣaṇa — the exact threat of dm-5-70, now an order), “kill any rescuer, god or not” — ego criminalises help itself. Temptation bullied (Ch5); now it legislates. Mark the escalation: proposal → threat → standing order.
3. **Huṅkāra-bhasma**: one wrathful seed-syllable ashes him — as Cāmara’s dart fell to huṅkāra (dm-3-1). Speech from integration is weapon; speech from smoke is noise. The verse teaches economy: established awareness needs no duel with deluded perception — a single syllable suffices. Then courage (siṃha) mops up the sixty thousand (dm-6-10).`,
