    devanagari: 'ऋषिरुवाच ।\nचण्डे च निहते दैत्ये मुण्डे च विनिपातिते ।\nबहुलेषु च सैन्येषु क्षयितेष्वसुरेश्वरः ॥१॥\nततः कोपपराधीनचेताः शुम्भः प्रतापवान् ।\nउद्योगं सर्वसैन्यानां दैत्यानामादिदेश ह ॥२॥\nअद्य सर्वबलैर्दैत्याः षडशीतिरुदायुधाः ।\nकम्बूनां चतुराशीतिर्निर्यान्तु स्वबलैर्वृताः ॥३॥\nकोटिवीर्याणि पञ्चाशदसुराणां कुलानि वै ।\nशतं कुलानि धूम्राणां निर्गच्छन्तु ममाज्ञया ॥४॥\nकालका दौर्हृदा मौर्याः कालकेयास्तथासुराः ।\nयुद्धाय सज्जा निर्यान्तु आज्ञया त्वरिता मम ॥५॥\nइत्याज्ञाप्यासुरपतिः शुम्भो भैरवशासनः ।\nनिर्जगाम महासैन्यसहस्त्रैर्बहुभिर्वृतः ॥६॥\nआयान्तं चण्डिका दृष्ट्वा तत्सैन्यमतिभीषणम् ।\nज्यास्वनैः पूरयामास धरणीगगनान्तरम् ॥७॥\nततः सिंहो महानादमतीव कृतवान्नृप ।\nघण्टास्वनेन तन्नादमम्बिका चोपबृंहयत् ॥८॥\nधनुसिंहघण्टानां नादापूरितदिङ्मुखा ।\nनिनादैर्भूषणैः काली जिग्ये विस्तारितानना ॥९॥\nतन्निनादमुपश्रुत्य दैत्यसैन्यैश्चतुर्दिशम् ।\nदेवी सिंहस्तथा काली सरोषैः परिवारिताः ॥१०॥',
    iast: 'ṛṣiruvāca |\ncaṇḍe ca nihate daitye muṇḍe ca vinipātite |\nbahuleṣu ca sainyeṣu kṣayiteṣvasureśvaraḥ ||1||\ntataḥ kopaparādhīnacetāḥ śumbhaḥ pratāpavān |\nudyogaṃ sarvasainyānāṃ daityānāmādideśa ha ||2||\nadya sarvabalairdaityāḥ ṣaḍaśītirudāyudhāḥ |\nkambūnāṃ caturāśītirniryāntu svabalairvṛtāḥ ||3||\nkoṭivīryāṇi pañcāśadasurāṇāṃ kulāni vai |\nśataṃ kulāni dhūmrāṇāṃ nirgacchantu mamājñayā ||4||\nkālakā daurhṛdā mauryāḥ kālakeyāstathāsurāḥ |\nyuddhāya sajjā niryāntu ājñayā tvaritā mama ||5||\nityājñāpyāsurapatiḥ śumbho bhairavaśāsanaḥ |\nnirjagāma mahāsainyasahastrairbahubhirvṛtaḥ ||6||\nāyāntaṃ caṇḍikā dṛṣṭvā tatsainyamatibhīṣaṇam |\njyāsvanaiḥ pūrayāmāsa dharaṇīgaganāntaram ||7||\ntataḥ siṃho mahānādamatīva kṛtavānnṛpa |\nghaṇṭāsvanena tannādamambikā copabṛṃhayat ||8||\ndhanusiṃhaghaṇṭānāṃ nādāpūritadiṅmukhā |\nninādairbhūṣaṇaiḥ kālī jigye vistāritānanā ||9||\ntanninādamupaśrutya daityasainyaiścaturdiśam |\ndevī siṃhastathā kālī saroṣaiḥ parivāritāḥ ||10||',
    conceptIds: ['shumbha-nishumbha', 'sadhana-samara', 'cit-shakti'],
    translation: 'The Ṛṣi said: When Caṇḍa was slain and Muṇḍa laid low, and many armies destroyed — then Śumbha the demon-lord, glorious, his mind enslaved by rage, ordered the mustering of all demon hosts. “This day let eighty-six demon clans march with all their forces, weapons raised; let eighty-four Kambu clans march girt with their armies. Let fifty Vīrya-koṭi demon tribes, and a hundred Smoke-clans, march at my command. Kālakās, Dauhradas, Mauryas, Kālakeyas — ready for war, march swiftly at my word.” So commanding, demon-lord Śumbha of dread edicts marched forth ringed with thousands of great armies. Seeing that most dread army come, Caṇḍikā filled earth-and-sky between with bowstring-twangs. Then the lion uttered a mighty roar, O king, and Ambikā swelled it with bell-clang. Kālī conquered — vast-mouthed, Her quarters filled with the din of bow, lion and bells, with roars and jingling ornaments. Hearing that din, from all four quarters Devī, lion and Kālī were ringed by wrathful demon hosts.',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (ego musters infinite reserves; integrated sound answers):**
1. **The clan-catalogue**: eighty-six, eighty-four, fifty koṭis, a hundred — Kālakās, Dauhradas, Mauryas, Kālakeyas. Ego’s reserves feel infinite when counted; Śāntanavī treats each name as a distinct excuse-tribe. For beginners: your faults also come in clans (food-clan, phone-clan, praise-clan) — list them as Śumbha lists his; counted foes are half-beaten.
2. **Nominative pendens (v1)**: “asureśvaraḥ” hangs grammatically into verse 2 (Śumbha orders) — Śāntanavī’s anvaya confirms. The slain past (Caṇḍa-Muṇḍa) grammatically drags its subject into the next assault: yesterday’s victories must be re-won; each chapter’s dead fertilise the next battle.
3. **Integrated sound vs scattered noise**: bowstring (resolve taut), lion-roar (courage), bell (alertness), Kālī’s ornament-din (terror transfigured) — four sounds filling earth-sky, answered from four quarters by encirclement. Pressure from all sides precedes breakthrough: when practice ripens, the world seems to close in — that is the ring before the rout. Next (dm-8-11): the Mothers emerge meanwhile.`,
