    devanagari: 'एतस्मिन्नन्तरे भूप विनाशाय सुरद्विषाम् ।\nभवायामरसिंहानामतिवीर्यबलान्विताः ॥११॥\nब्रह्मेशगुहविष्णूनां तथैवेन्द्रस्य शक्तयः ।\nशरीरेभ्यो विनिष्क्रम्य तद्रूपैश्चण्डिकां ययुः ॥१२॥\nयस्य देवस्य यद्रूपं यथाभूषणवाहनम् ।\nतद्वदेव हि तच्छक्तिरसुरान्योद्धुमाययौ ॥१३॥\nहंसयुक्तविमानाग्रे साक्षसूत्रकमण्डलुः ।\nआयाता ब्रह्मणः शक्तिर्ब्रह्माणी साभिधीयते ॥१४॥\nमाहेश्वरी वृषारूढा त्रिशूलवरधारिणी ।\nमहाहिवलया प्राप्ता चन्द्ररेखाविभूषणा ॥१५॥\nकौमारी शक्तिहस्ता च मयूरवरवाहना ।\nयोद्धुमभ्याययौ दैत्यानम्बिका गुहरूपिणी ॥१६॥\nतथैव वैष्णवी शक्तिर्गरुडोपरि संस्थिता ।\nशङ्खचक्रगदाशार्ङ्गखड्गहस्ताभ्युपाययौ ॥१७॥\nयज्ञवाराहमतुलं रूपं या बिभ्रतो हरेः ।\nशक्तिः साप्याययौ तत्र वाराहीं बिभ्रती तनुम् ॥१८॥\nनारसिंही नृसिंहस्य बिभ्रती सदृशं वपुः ।\nप्राप्ता तत्र सटाक्षेपक्षिप्तनक्षत्रसंहतिः ॥१९॥\nवज्रहस्ता तथैवैन्द्री गजराजोपरि स्थिता ।\nप्राप्ता सहस्रनयना यथा शक्रस्तथैव सा ॥२०॥',
    iast: 'etasminnantare bhūpa vināśāya suradviṣām |\nbhavāyāmarasiṃhānāmativīryabalānvitāḥ ||11||\nbrahmeśaguhaviṣṇūnāṃ tathaivendrasya śaktayaḥ |\nśarīrebhyo viniṣkramya tadrūpaiścaṇḍikāṃ yayuḥ ||12||\nyasya devasya yadrūpaṃ yathābhūṣaṇavāhanam |\ntadvadeva hi tacchaktirasurānyoddhumāyayau ||13||\nhaṃsayuktavimānāgre sākṣasūtrakamaṇḍaluḥ |\nāyātā brahmaṇaḥ śaktirbrahmāṇī sābhidhīyate ||14||\nmāheśvarī vṛṣārūḍhā triśūlavaradhāriṇī |\nmahāhivalayā prāptā candrarekhāvibhūṣaṇā ||15||\nkaumārī śaktihastā ca mayūravaravāhanā |\nyoddhumabhyāyayau daityānambikā guharūpiṇī ||16||\ntathaiva vaiṣṇavī śaktirgaruḍopari saṃsthitā |\nśaṅkhacakragadāśārṅgakhaḍgahastābhyupāyayau ||17||\nyajñavārāhamatulaṃ rūpaṃ yā bibhrato hareḥ |\nśaktiḥ sāpyāyayau tatra vārāhīṃ bibhratī tanum ||18||\nnārasiṃhī nṛsiṃhasya bibhratī sadṛśaṃ vapuḥ |\nprāptā tatra saṭākṣepakṣiptanakṣatrasaṃhatiḥ ||19||\nvajrahastā tathaivaindrī gajarājopari sthitā |\nprāptā sahasranayanā yathā śakrastathaiva sā ||20||',
    conceptIds: ['matrikas', 'cit-shakti', 'sadhana-samara'],
    translation: '“Meanwhile, O king — for demons’ ruin and god-lions’ weal — the exceeding-mighty: from Brahmā, Śiva, Skanda, Viṣṇu and Indra their śaktis issued from their bodies and joined Caṇḍikā in matching forms. In just such form, ornaments and vehicle as each god’s, his śakti went to fight the demons. Seated on a swan-drawn car’s prow, rosary and water-pot in hand, came Brahmā’s śakti, called Brahmāṇī. Māheśvarī came bull-mounted, bearing the excellent trident, serpent-braceleted, moon-streak adorned. Kaumārī, spear in hand, on a fine peacock, came as Ambikā in Guha’s form to fight the demons. Likewise Vaiṣṇavī, stationed on Garuḍa, came up with conch, discus, mace, bow and sword in hand. The śakti who bore Hari’s peerless sacrifice-boar form came there too, wearing a boar body as Vārāhī. Nārasiṃhī came wearing Nṛsiṃha’s like form — star-clusters flung by Her mane-shakes arriving there. Vajra-handed Aindrī too, throned on the elephant-king, came thousand-eyed — as Śakra, so She.”',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (meanwhile: faculties personified as goddesses):**
1. **Etasminnantare — help mid-crisis**: the Mothers emerge WHILE ringed (v10 → v11 “meanwhile”), not after deliverance. Grace reinforces mid-battle. “God-lions” (amarasiṃhāḥ, best of gods): help comes to the best in you while the worst still rings you round.
2. **Śakti-śaktimator unity**: Nāgojī — śaktis are “non-different goddesses” (svavyatirekiṇyo devatāḥ? no — svātirekiṇyaḥ? His point: śakti and śaktimat share being); Caturdharī: “Viṣṇu” here gathers Varāha-Nṛsiṃha too. Each faculty, pressed to extremity, reveals its feminine divine person: study becomes Brahmāṇī (rosary-waterpot), stillness Māheśvarī (bull-trident-moon), discipline Kaumārī (spear-peacock), preservation Vaiṣṇavī (five weapons), rootedness Vārāhī (boar), fierce protection Nārasiṃhī (mane shaking stars), sovereign will Aindrī (thousand-eyed). For beginners: meet your own faculties as Mothers, not mechanics.
3. **Matching forms (tadrūpaiḥ)**: help arrives in the shape of the helped — the teaching suits the taught. Caṇḍikā (Kauśikī) is their rally-point: all shaktis converge on the central Mother, as all practices converge in surrender. Next (dm-8-21): Śiva Himself is deputised — and named for it.`,
