    id: 'dm-9-7',
    number: '9.7-18',
    section: 'Uttama Carita (Rudra-Granthi Bheda / Śumbha-Niśumbha Vadha)',
    devanagari: 'ततो युद्धमतीवासीद्देव्या शुम्भनिशुम्भयोः ।\nशरवर्षमतीवोग्रं मेघयोरिव वर्षतोः ॥७॥\nचिच्छेदास्तांश्छरांस्ताभ्यां चण्डिका स्वशरोत्करैः ।\nताडयामास चाङ्गेषु शस्त्रौघैरसुरेश्वरौ ॥८॥\nनिशुम्भो निशितं खड्गं चर्म चादाय सुप्रभम् ।\nअताडयन्मूर्ध्नि सिंह देव्या वाहनमुत्तमम् ॥९॥\nताडिते वाहने देवी क्षुरप्रेणासिमुत्तमम् ।\nनिशुम्भस्याशु चिच्छेद चर्म चाप्यष्टचन्द्रकम् ॥१०॥\nछिन्ने चर्मणि खड्गे च शक्तिं चिक्षेप सोऽसुरः ।\nतामप्यस्य द्विधा चक्रे चक्रेणाभिमुखागताम् ॥११॥\nकोपाध्मातो निशुम्भोऽथ शूलं जग्राह दानवः ।\nआयान्तं मुष्टिपातेन देवी तच्चाप्यचूर्णयत् ॥१२॥\nआविध्याथ गदां सोऽपि चिक्षेप चण्डिकां प्रति ।\nसापि देव्या त्रिशूलेन भिन्ना भस्मत्वमागता ॥१३॥\nततः परशुहस्तं तमायान्तं दैत्यपुङ्गवम् ।\nआहत्य देवी बाणौधैरपातयत भूतले ॥१४॥\nतस्मिन्निपतिते भूमौ निशुम्भे भीमविक्रमे ।\nभ्रातर्यतीव संक्रुद्धः प्रययौ योद्धुमम्बिकाम् ॥१५॥\nस रथस्थस्तथात्युच्चैर्गृहीतपरमायुधैः ।\nभुजैरष्टाभिरतुलैर्व्याप्याशेषं बभौ नभः ॥१६॥\nतमायान्तं समालोक्य देवी शङ्खमवादयत् ।\nज्याशब्दं चापि धनुषश्चकारातीव दुःसहम् ॥१७॥\nपूरयामास ककुभो निजघण्टास्वनेन च ।\nसमस्तदैत्यसैन्यानां तेजोवधविधायिना ॥१८॥',
    iast: 'tato yuddhamatīvāsīddevyā śumbhaniśumbhayoḥ |\nśaravarṣamatīvograṃ meghayoriva varṣatoḥ ||7||\ncicchedāstāṃścharāṃstābhyāṃ caṇḍikā svaśarotkaraiḥ |\ntāḍayāmāsa cāṅgeṣu śastraughairasureśvarau ||8||\nniśumbho niśitaṃ khaḍgaṃ carma cādāya suprabham |\natāḍayanmūrdhni siṃha devyā vāhanamuttamam ||9||\ntāḍite vāhane devī kṣurapreṇāsimuttamam |\nniśumbhasyāśu ciccheda carma cāpyaṣṭacandrakam ||10||\nchinne carmaṇi khaḍge ca śaktiṃ cikṣepa so\'suraḥ |\ntāmapyasya dvidhā cakre cakreṇābhimukhāgatām ||11||\nkopādhmāto niśumbho\'tha śūlaṃ jagrāha dānavaḥ |\nāyāntaṃ muṣṭipātena devī taccāpyacūrṇayat ||12||\nāvidhyātha gadāṃ so\'pi cikṣepa caṇḍikāṃ prati |\nsāpi devyā triśūlena bhinnā bhasmatvamāgatā ||13||\ntataḥ paraśuhastaṃ tamāyāntaṃ daityapuṅgavam |\nāhatya devī bāṇaudhairapātayata bhūtale ||14||\ntasminnipatite bhūmau niśumbhe bhīmavikrame |\nbhrātaryatīva saṃkruddhaḥ prayayau yoddhumambikām ||15||\nsa rathasthastathātyuccairgṛhītaparamāyudhaiḥ |\nbhujairaṣṭābhiratulairvyāpyāśeṣaṃ babhau nabhaḥ ||16||\ntamāyāntaṃ samālokya devī śaṅkhamavādayat |\njyāśabdaṃ cāpi dhanuṣaścakārātīva duḥsaham ||17||\npūrayāmāsa kakubho nijaghaṇṭāsvanena ca |\nsamastadaityasainyānāṃ tejovadhavidhāyinā ||18||',
    conceptIds: ['shumbha-nishumbha', 'sadhana-samara'],
    translation: 'Then a most exceeding battle raged between Devī and Śumbha-Niśumbha — arrow-rains most dread, like two raining clouds. Caṇḍikā cut their arrows with arrow-masses and smote the two demon-lords’ limbs with weapon-floods. Niśumbha seized a whetted sword and gleaming shield and smote Devī’s noble vehicle the lion on the head. When the vehicle was struck, Devī swiftly cut Niśumbha’s excellent sword with Her razor-edge, and his shield bright with eight moons. Sword and shield shorn, the demon hurled a javelin — that too, aimed at Her, She split in two with Her discus. Swollen with rage, Niśumbha seized a spear; as it came, Devī shattered it too with a fist-blow. Whirling, he flung his mace at Caṇḍikā — split by Devī’s trident, it came to ash. Then the demon-chief came axe in hand; Devī struck him down to earth with arrow-floods. When dread-valiant Niśumbha fell to earth, his brother in exceeding rage marched to fight Ambikā — chariot-borne, on high, grasping supreme weapons, filling all the sky with eight unequalled arms, he shone. Seeing him come, Devī blew Her conch and made Her bow’s twang unbearable; She filled the quarters with Her own bell-sound — that murders the brilliance of all demon hosts.',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (arsenal stripped piece by piece; sound that murders brilliance):**
1. **Two raining clouds (meghayor iva)**: both sides storm — the even battle before grace tips it. Niśumbha’s escalating arsenal (sword-shield → javelin → spear → mace → axe) is stripped piece by piece, each broken closer to the body: bow cut, eight-moon shield cut, javelin discus-split, spear fist-shattered, mace trident-ashed. For beginners: watch practice disarm one weapon at a time — shield-excuses first, then pet spears, then the axe-arguments; impatience wants all at once.
2. **Eight-moon shield**: aṣṭacandraka — the eightfold lunar mind (five senses + manas + buddhi + ahaṅkāra?) gilded; razor-edge (kṣurapra, the keenest discernment) shears it. Whatever gilds the ego-mind, however moon-bright, is cut.
3. **Tejovadha sound**: conch (prāṇa-proclamation), bow-twang (taut readiness), HER bell (alertness that murders brilliance — tejovadha: enemy-lustre slain by sound alone). Śumbha’s advent with eight unequalled arms filling the sky — asmitā’s omnipotence-display before its fall (dm-10-1). The duel proper now his alone.`,
    keyPoints: [
      '**Stripped Piece by Piece**: Shield-excuses, pet spears, axe-arguments — disarmed closer and closer.',
      '**Moon-bright Shield Cut**: Whatever gilds the ego-mind, however luminous, is sheared.',
      '**Sound Murdering Brilliance**: Conch, bow-twang, bell — enemy-lustre slain by sound alone.'
    ]
