    devanagari: 'ततः सिंहो महानादैस्त्याजितेभमहामदैः ।\nपूरयामास गगनं गां तथोपदिशो दश ॥१९॥\nततः काली समुत्पत्य गगनं क्ष्मामताडयत् ।\nकराभ्यां तन्निनादेन प्राक्स्वनास्ते तिरोहिताः ॥२०॥\nअट्टाट्टहासमशिवं शिवदूती चकार ह ।\nतैः शब्दैरसुरास्त्रेसुः शुम्भः कोपं परं ययौ ॥२१॥\nदुरात्मंस्तिष्ठ तिष्ठेति व्याजहाराम्बिका यदा ।\nतदा जयेत्यभिहितं देवैराकाशसंस्थितैः ॥२२॥\nशुम्भेनागत्य या शक्तिर्मुक्ता ज्वालातिभीषणा ।\nआयान्ती वह्निकूटाभा सा निरस्ता महोल्कया ॥२३॥\nसिंहनादेन शुम्भस्य व्याप्तं लोकत्रयान्तरम् ।\nनिर्घातनिःस्वनो घोरो जितवानवनीपते ॥२४॥\nशुम्भमुक्ताशरान्देवी शुम्भस्तत्प्रहिताशरान् ।\nचिच्छेद स्वशरैरुग्रैः शतशोऽथ सहस्रशः ॥२५॥\nततः सा चण्डिका क्रुद्धा शूलेनाभिजघान तम् ।\nस तदाभिहतो भूमौ मूर्च्छितो निपपात ह ॥२६॥',
    iast: 'tataḥ siṃho mahānādaistyājitebhamahāmadaiḥ |\npūrayāmāsa gaganaṃ gāṃ tathopadiśo daśa ||19||\ntataḥ kālī samutpatya gaganaṃ kṣmāmatāḍayat |\nkarābhyāṃ tanninādena prāksvanāste tirohitāḥ ||20||\naṭṭāṭṭahāsamaśivaṃ śivadūtī cakāra ha |\ntaiḥ śabdairasurāstresuḥ śumbhaḥ kopaṃ paraṃ yayau ||21||\ndurātmaṃstiṣṭha tiṣṭheti vyājahārāmbikā yadā |\ntadā jayetyabhihitaṃ devairākāśasaṃsthitaiḥ ||22||\nśumbhenāgatya yā śaktirmuktā jvālātibhīṣaṇā |\nāyāntī vahnikūṭābhā sā nirastā maholkayā ||23||\nsiṃhanādena śumbhasya vyāptaṃ lokatrayāntaram |\nnirghātaniḥsvano ghoro jitavānavanīpate ||24||\nśumbhamuktāśarāndevī śumbhastatprahitāśarān |\nciccheda svaśarairugraiḥ śataśo\'tha sahasraśaḥ ||25||\ntataḥ sā caṇḍikā kruddhā śūlenābhijaghāna tam |\nsa tadābhihato bhūmau mūrcchito nipapāta ha ||26||',
    conceptIds: ['shumbha-nishumbha', 'sadhana-samara'],
    translation: 'Then the lion with mighty roars that stripped rut-elephants of frenzy filled the sky, the earth and the ten quarters. Then Kālī leapt into the sky and smote the earth with both hands; by that din the former sounds were drowned. Śivadūtī uttered a wild inauspicious guffaw; at those sounds the demons trembled, and Śumbha reached supreme rage. When Ambikā cried “Stay, stay, O evil soul!” — then “Victory!” was called by the gods stationed in the sky. The missile loosed by Śumbha closing in, exceeding-dread with flames — coming like a fire-mass, it was repelled by Maholkā (the great firebrand). With Śumbha’s lion-roar the triple world’s midst was pervaded — but a dread portent-crash out-roared it, O king. The arrows Śumbha loosed, Devī cut, and Śumbha cut Hers — each with fierce arrows, by hundreds, by thousands. Then wrathful Caṇḍikā struck him with Her spear; so struck, he fell swooning to earth.',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (sound-war; even battle; the swoon):**
1. **Antiphonal war-cries**: lion’s frenzy-stripping roars fill ten quarters; Kālī’s earth-slap drowns all prior sounds (prāksvanās tirohitāḥ — when the Void claps, earlier noises vanish); Śivadūtī’s inauspicious guffaw (aśiva — grace wears the dread mask) terrifies; “tiṣṭha tiṣṭha” answered by heaven’s “jaya!” Challenge and cheer in antiphony — every bold stand draws heaven’s applause; act, and the gods call victory.
2. **Maholkā repels**: Śumbha’s flame-dread śakti-missile, like a fire-mass, is voided by Maholkā (the great firebrand — Nāgojī and Guptavatī: Her equal-or-namesake power). Fire answers fire; then roar answers roar — his lion-roar filling worlds is out-roared by portent-crash (nirghāta, heaven’s own thunder, says Damasoddhara). For beginners: meet intensity with intensity, not suppression.
3. **Perfectly even duel, then the spear**: śataśaḥ-sahasraśaḥ (hundreds-thousands mutual cutting — Nāgojī: “samayuddham,” an even battle) — equality before the fall. Then wrathful Caṇḍikā’s spear swoons (mūrcchita, not slays!) him — Niśumbha will rise again (dm-9-27). Swoons precede deaths: insights fell the ego repeatedly before finally.`,
    keyPoints: [
      '**Challenge Draws Applause**: Bold “tiṣṭha!” draws heaven’s “jaya!” — act, and gods cheer.',
      '**Fire Answers Fire**: Meet intensity with intensity — Maholkā voids flame-missiles.',
      '**Swoons Precede Deaths**: Insights fell the ego repeatedly before finally.'
    ]
