    devanagari: 'ततो निशुम्भः सम्प्राप्य चेतनामात्तकार्मुकः ।\nआजघान शरैर्देवीं कालीं केसरिणं तथा ॥२७॥\nपुनश्च कृत्वा बाहूनामयुतं दनुजेश्वरः ।\nचक्रायुतेन दितिजश्छादयामास चण्डिकाम् ॥२८॥\nततो भगवती क्रुद्धा दुर्गा दुर्गार्तिनाशिनी ।\nचिच्छेद तानि चक्राणि स्वशरैः सायकांश्च तान् ॥२९॥\nततो निशुम्भो वेगेन गदामादाय चण्डिकाम् ।\nअभ्यधावत वै हन्तुं दैत्यसेनासमावृतः ॥३०॥\nतस्यापतत एवाशु गदां चिच्छेद चण्डिका ।\nखड्गेन शितधारेण स च शूलं समाददे ॥३१॥\nशूलहस्तं समायान्तं निशुम्भममरार्दनम् ।\nहृदि विव्याध शूलेन वेगाविद्धेन चण्डिका ॥३२॥\nभिन्नस्य तस्य शूलेन हृदयान्निःसृतोऽपरः ।\nमहाबलो महावीर्यस्तिष्ठेति पुरुषो वदन् ॥३३॥\nतस्य निष्क्रामतो देवी प्रहस्य स्वनवत्ततः ।\nशिरश्चिच्छेद खड्गेन ततोऽसावपतद्भुवि ॥३४॥\nततः सिंहश्चखाद दंष्ट्राक्षुण्णशिरोधरान् ।\nअसुरांस्तांस्तदा काली शिवदूती तथा परान् ॥३५॥\nकौमारीशक्तिनिर्भिन्नाः केचिन्नेशुर्महासुराः ।\nब्रह्माणीमन्त्रपूतेन तोयेनान्ये निराकृताः ॥३६॥\nमाहेश्वरीत्रिशूलेन भिन्ना पेतुस्तथापरे ।\nवाराहीतुण्डघातेन केचिच्चूर्णकृता भुवि ॥३७॥\nखण्डं खण्डं च चक्रेण वैष्णव्या दानवाः कृताः ।\nवज्रेण चैन्द्रीहस्ताग्रविमुक्तेन तथा परे ॥३८॥\nकेचिद्विनेशुरसुराः केचिन्नष्टा महाहवात् ।\nभक्षिताश्चापरे कालीशिवदूतीमृगाधिपैः ॥३९॥',
    iast: 'tato niśumbhaḥ samprāpya cetanāmāttakārmukaḥ |\nājaghāna śarairdevīṃ kālīṃ kesariṇaṃ tathā ||27||\npunaśca kṛtvā bāhūnāmayutaṃ danujeśvaraḥ |\ncakrāyutena ditijaśchādayāmāsa caṇḍikām ||28||\ntato bhagavatī kruddhā durgā durgārtināśinī |\nciccheda tāni cakrāṇi svaśaraiḥ sāyakāṃśca tān ||29||\ntato niśumbho vegena gadāmādāya caṇḍikām |\nabhyadhāvata vai hantuṃ daityasenāsamāvṛtaḥ ||30||\ntasyāpatata evāśu gadāṃ ciccheda caṇḍikā |\nkhaḍgena śitadhāreṇa sa ca śūlaṃ samādade ||31||\nśūlahastaṃ samāyāntaṃ niśumbhamamarārdanam |\nhṛdi vivyādha śūlena vegāviddhena caṇḍikā ||32||\nbhinnasya tasya śūlena hṛdayānniḥsṛto\'paraḥ |\nmahābalo mahāvīryastiṣṭheti puruṣo vadan ||33||\ntasya niṣkrāmato devī prahasya svanavattataḥ |\nśiraściccheda khaḍgena tato\'sāvapatadbhuvi ||34||\ntataḥ siṃhaścakhāda daṃṣṭrākṣuṇṇaśirodharān |\nasurāṃstāṃstadā kālī śivadūtī tathā parān ||35||\nkaumārīśaktinirbhinnāḥ kecinneśurmahāsurāḥ |\nbrahmāṇīmantrapūtena toyenānye nirākṛtāḥ ||36||\nmāheśvarītriśūlena bhinnā petustathāpare |\nvārāhītuṇḍaghātena keciccūrṇakṛtā bhuvi ||37||\nkhaṇḍaṃ khaṇḍaṃ ca cakreṇa vaiṣṇavyā dānavāḥ kṛtāḥ |\nvajreṇa caindrīhastāgravimuktena tathā pare ||38||\nkecidvineśurasurāḥ kecinnaṣṭā mahāhavāt |\nbhakṣitāścāpare kālīśivadūtīmṛgādhipaiḥ ||39||',
    conceptIds: ['shumbha-nishumbha', 'matrikas', 'sadhana-samara'],
    translation: 'Then Niśumbha, regaining consciousness, bow in hand, smote Devī, Kālī and the lion with arrows. And making ten thousand arms, the demon-lord veiled Caṇḍikā with ten thousand discs. Then wrathful Bhagavatī Durgā, destroyer of the distressed’s distress, cut those discs and arrows with Her shafts. Then Niśumbha seized his mace and charged Caṇḍikā to slay Her, ringed with demon armies. As he rushed, Caṇḍikā swiftly cut his mace with Her keen-edged sword — and he seized a spear. As spear-armed Niśumbha, tormentor of gods, came on, Caṇḍikā pierced his heart with a force-hurled spear. From his spear-pierced heart came forth another — a most strong, most valiant man crying “Stay!” Laughing loudly, Devī cut his head with Her sword as he emerged — and he fell to earth. Then the lion devoured the fang-crushed-necked demons; Kālī, Śivadūtī and the rest devoured those demons. Some great demons, pierced by Kaumārī’s spear, perished; others were repelled by Brahmāṇī’s mantra-hallowed water. Felled by Māheśvarī’s trident fell others; by Vārāhī’s snout-blows some were powdered on the ground. To pieces by Vaiṣṇavī’s discus were demons cut, and others by the bolt loosed from Aindrī’s hand-tip. Some demons perished, some fled the great fray, others were eaten by Kālī, Śivadūtī and the lion-king.',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (revival, ten thousand arms, the heart’s backup, the feast):**
1. **Swoon ≠ death**: Niśumbha “regaining consciousness” (samprāpya cetanām) returns deadlier — ten thousand arms, ten thousand discs veiling Caṇḍikā. Insights swoon the ego; only the heart-pierce kills. For beginners: when a “dead” fault returns stronger, you only swooned it — strike at the heart (motive), not the arms (behaviours).
2. **The heart’s backup man**: pierced in the heart, Niśumbha births ANOTHER crying “tiṣṭha!” — the backup ego, the observer that survives self-attack (“I who watch myself being pierced”). Laughing (prahasya — joy, not effort), She beheads the emergent one mid-emergence. Watch for the second man: the “spiritual” self that narrates its own piercing.
3. **Durgā Durgārtināśinī**: Bhagavatī as “destroyer of the distressed’s distress” — the Name for this work. Then the Mothers’ feast (v35-39): lion crunches, Kālī-Śivadūtī eat, Kaumārī pierces, Brahmāṇī’s mantra-water repels (holy water for bewitched faults!), Māheśvarī fells, Vārāhī powders, Vaiṣṇavī pieces, Aindrī bolts — perish, flee, or be eaten: the only three fates of faults. Niśumbha (“mine”) is dead; bare “I” (Śumbha) stands alone (Ch10).`,
