    devanagari: 'शरवर्षैः शितैः शस्त्रैस्तथास्त्रैश्चैव दारुणैः ।\nतयोर्युद्धमभूद्यत् सर्वलोकभयङ्करम् ॥७॥\nदिव्यान्यस्त्राणि शतशो मुमुचे यान्यथाम्बिका ।\nबभञ्ज तानि दैत्येन्द्रस्तत्प्रतीघातकर्तृभिः ॥८॥\nमुक्तानि तेन चास्त्राणि दिव्यानि परमेश्वरी ।\nबभञ्ज लीलयैवोग्रहुङ्कारोच्चारणादिभिः ॥९॥\nततः शरशतैर्देवीमाच्छादयत सोऽसुरः ।\nसा च देवी तत्कुपिता धनुश्चिच्छेद चेषुभिः ॥१०॥\nछिन्ने धनुषि दैत्येन्द्रस्तथा शक्तिमथाददे ।\nचिच्छेद देवी चक्रेण तामप्यस्य करे स्थिताम् ॥११॥\nततः खड्गमुपादाय शतचन्द्रं च भानुमत् ।\nअभ्यधावत तां देवीं दैत्यानामधिपेश्वरः ॥१२॥\nतस्यापतत एवाशु खड्गं चिच्छेद चण्डिका ।\nधनुर्मुक्तैः शितैर्वाणैश्चर्म चार्ककरामलम् ॥१३॥\nहताश्वः स तदा दैत्यश्छिन्नधन्वा विसारथिः ।\nजग्राह मुद्गरं घोरमम्बिकानिधनोद्यतः ॥१४॥\nचिच्छेदापततस्तस्य मुद्गरं निशितैः शरैः ।\nतथापि सोऽभ्यधावत्तां मुष्टिमुद्यम्य वेगवान् ॥१५॥\nस मुष्टिं पातयामास हृदये दैत्यपुङ्गवः ।\nदेव्यास्तं चापि सा देवी तलेनोरस्यताडयत् ॥१६॥\nतलप्रहाराभिहतो निपपात महीतले ।\nस दैत्यराजः सहसा पुनरेव तथोत्थितः ॥१७॥\nउत्पत्य च प्रगृह्योच्चैर्देवीं गगनमास्थितः ।\nतत्रापि सा निराधारा युयुधे तेन चण्डिका ॥१८॥',
    iast: 'śaravarṣaiḥ śitaiḥ śastraistathāstraiścaiva dāruṇaiḥ |\ntayoryuddhamabhūdyat sarvalokabhayaṅkaram ||7||\ndivyānyastrāṇi śataśo mumuce yānyathāmbikā |\nbabhañja tāni daityendrastatpratīghātakartṛbhiḥ ||8||\nmuktāni tena cāstrāṇi divyāni parameśvarī |\nbabhañja līlayaivograhuṅkāroccāraṇādibhiḥ ||9||\ntataḥ śaraśatairdevīmācchādayata so\'suraḥ |\nsā ca devī tatkupitā dhanuściccheda ceṣubhiḥ ||10||\nchinne dhanuṣi daityendrastathā śaktimathādade |\nciccheda devī cakreṇa tāmapyasya kare sthitām ||11||\ntataḥ khaḍgamupādāya śatacandraṃ ca bhānumat |\nabhyadhāvata tāṃ devīṃ daityānāmadhipeśvaraḥ ||12||\ntasyāpatata evāśu khaḍgaṃ ciccheda caṇḍikā |\ndhanurmuktaiḥ śitairvāṇaiścarma cārkakarāmalam ||13||\nhatāśvaḥ sa tadā daityaśchinnadhanvā visārathiḥ |\njagrāha mudgaraṃ ghoramambikānidhanodyataḥ ||14||\ncicchedāpatatastasya mudgaraṃ niśitaiḥ śaraiḥ |\ntathāpi so\'bhyadhāvattāṃ muṣṭimudyamya vegavān ||15||\nsa muṣṭiṃ pātayāmāsa hṛdaye daityapuṅgavaḥ |\ndevyāstaṃ cāpi sā devī talenorasyatāḍayat ||16||\ntalaprahārābhihato nipapāta mahītale |\nsa daityarājaḥ sahasā punareva tathotthitaḥ ||17||\nutpatya ca pragṛhyoccairdevīṃ gaganamāsthitaḥ |\ntatrāpi sā nirādhārā yuyudhe tena caṇḍikā ||18||',
    conceptIds: ['shumbha-nishumbha', 'sadhana-samara'],
    translation: 'With sharp showers of missiles and dread weapons, a world-terrifying battle raged between the two. The divine weapons Ambikā loosed by hundreds, the demon-lord shattered with counter-strikers; the divine weapons he loosed, Parameśvarī shattered playfully with fierce huṅkāra-utterances and the like. Then the demon veiled Devī with arrow-hundreds; angered thereat, She cut his bow with arrows. Bow shorn, he seized a javelin — Devī cut that too, even in his hand, with Her discus. Seizing a moon-bright hundred-mooned sword, the demons’ emperor charged Her. As he rushed, Caṇḍikā swiftly cut his sword with bow-loosed keen shafts, and his sun-ray-spotless shield. Horseless, bowless, driverless, bent on Ambikā’s murder he seized a dread mace. She cut his rushing mace with whetted shafts — yet fist raised, he charged Her still. The demon-chief let his fist fall on Her heart — and She struck his chest with Her palm. Palm-smitten he dropped to earth — yet instantly the demon-king rose again. Leaping, seizing Devī, he mounted high into the sky; and there too, supportless, Caṇḍikā fought him.',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (disarmament to bare fists; the unsupported fight):**
1. **Even match, witnessed by worlds**: divine weapons met by counter-strikers (pratīghāta-kartṛ — ego always fields a counter); Hers loosed by hundreds, His answered in kind. Then the turn: She shatters playfully (līlayā) with huṅkāra-utterances — sound-weapons cost Her nothing. For beginners: answer intensity with ease, not strain; strain is his element.
2. **Stripped to bare hands**: bow → javelin (cut even in his hand!) → hundred-mooned sword + sun-spotless shield → mace → RAISED FIST. Ego disarmed stage by stage, yet charging still — fists are asmitā’s last argument: bare assertion without instrument. His fist falls on Her HEART (hṛdaye); Her palm answers on HIS chest (urasi) — heart answers heart; the Mother meets the final blow at its own level and returns it transmuted.
3. **Nirādhārā**: seized and lifted skyless? — no: lifted SKYWARD, “there too, supportless (nirādhārā), Caṇḍikā fought him.” Pure consciousness needs no ground, no standpoint, no premise — even lifted off every position, She fights on. When argument removes all your ground, fight groundless: positions are his need, not Hers. Next (dm-10-19): the whirl, the fall, the settling.`,
    keyPoints: [
      '**Answer Ease with Ease**: His intensity, Her play — strain is ego’s element, never meet him there.',
      '**Stripped to Fists**: Bow, spear, sword, mace — asmitā’s last argument is bare assertion.',
      '**Fight Groundless**: Nirādhārā — consciousness needs no standpoint; positions are his need.'
    ]
