    section: 'Uttama Carita (Rudra-Granthi Bheda / Śumbha-Niśumbha Vadha)',
    devanagari: 'नियुद्धं खे तदा दैत्यश्चण्डिका च परस्परम् ।\nचक्रतुः प्रथमं युद्ध मुनिविस्मयकारकम् ॥१९॥\nततो नियुद्धं सुचिरं कृत्वा तेनाम्बिका सह ।\nउत्पात्य भ्रामयामास चिक्षेप धरणीतले ॥२०॥\nस क्षिप्तो धरणीं प्राप्य मुष्टिमुद्यम्य वेगतः ।\nअभ्यधावत दुष्टात्मा चण्डिकानिधनेच्छया ॥२१॥\nतमायान्तं ततो देवी सर्वदैत्यजनेश्वरम् ।\nजगत्यां पातयामास भित्त्वा शूलेन वक्षसि ॥२२॥\nस गतासुः पपातोर्व्यां देवीशूलाग्रविक्षतः ।\nचालयन्सकलां पृथ्वीं साब्धिद्वीपां सपर्वताम् ॥२३॥\nउत्पातमेघाः सोल्का ये प्रागासंस्ते शमं ययुः ।\nसरितो मार्गवाहिन्यस्तथासंस्तत्र पातिते ॥२४॥\nततः प्रसन्नमखिलं हते तस्मिन्दुरात्मनि ।\nजगत्स्वास्थ्यमतीवाप निर्मलं चाभवन्नभः ॥२५॥\nततो देवगणाः सर्वे हर्षनिर्भरमानसाः ।\nबभूवुर्निहते तस्मिन् गन्धर्वा ललितं जगुः ॥२६॥\nअवादयंस्तथैवान्ये ननृतुश्चाप्सरोगणाः ।\nववुः पुण्यास्तथा वाताः सुप्रभोऽभूद्दिवाकरः ॥२७॥\nजज्वलुश्चाग्नयः शान्ताः शान्ता दिग्जनितस्वनाः ॥२८॥',
    iast: 'niyuddhaṃ khe tadā daityaścaṇḍikā ca parasparam |\ncakratuḥ prathamaṃ yuddha munivismayakārakam ||19||\ntato niyuddhaṃ suciraṃ kṛtvā tenāmbikā saha |\nutpātya bhrāmayāmāsa cikṣepa dharaṇītale ||20||\nsa kṣipto dharaṇīṃ prāpya muṣṭimudyamya vegataḥ |\nabhyadhāvata duṣṭātmā caṇḍikānidhanecchayā ||21||\ntamāyāntaṃ tato devī sarvadaityajaneśvaram |\njagatyāṃ pātayāmāsa bhittvā śūlena vakṣasi ||22||\nsa gatāsuḥ papātorvyāṃ devīśūlāgravikṣataḥ |\ncālayansakalāṃ pṛthvīṃ sābdhidvīpāṃ saparvatām ||23||\nutpātameghāḥ solkā ye prāgāsaṃste śamaṃ yayuḥ |\nsarito mārgavāhinyastathāsaṃstatra pātite ||24||\ntataḥ prasannamakhilaṃ hate tasmindurātmani |\njagatsvāsthyamatīvāpa nirmalaṃ cābhavannabhaḥ ||25||\ntato devagaṇāḥ sarve harṣanirbharamānasāḥ |\nbabhūvurnihate tasmin gandharvā lalitaṃ jaguḥ ||26||\navādayaṃstathaivānye nanṛtuścāpsarogaṇāḥ |\nvavuḥ puṇyāstathā vātāḥ suprabho\'bhūddivākaraḥ ||27||\njajvaluścāgnayaḥ śāntāḥ śāntā digjanitasvanāḥ ||28||',
    conceptIds: ['shumbha-nishumbha', 'sadhana-samara'],
    translation: 'Then demon and Caṇḍikā wrestled mutually in the sky — a first, sage-amazing bout. Long wrestling with him, Ambikā lifted, whirled and flung him to earth. Flung, reaching ground, fist raised, the evil soul charged, craving Caṇḍikā’s murder. As he came, Devī felled the lord of all demon-folk to earth, piercing his chest with Her spear. Lifeless he fell to earth, pierced by Devī’s spear-tip, shaking the whole earth with oceans, isles and mountains. The portent-clouds with meteors that had gathered now calmed; rivers flowed in their courses once more when he was laid low there. Then, with that evil soul slain, all the world grew clear and attained exceeding health; spotless became the sky. Then all the god-hosts, hearts filled with joy at his slaying — gandharvas sang sweetly, others played, apsarā troupes danced, pure winds blew, the sun shone fair, and calm fires blazed with quarter-echoing sounds.',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (the whirl, the fall, the settling — Rudra-granthi pierced):**
1. **Prathama yuddha**: a wrestling (niyuddha) bout — “first” of its kind, “sage-amazing” (muni-vismaya): even seers marvel when pure consciousness grapples bare asmitā body-to-body. Long bout (suciraṃ), then lift-whirl-fling (utpātya-bhrāmayāmāsa-cikṣepa): grace does not push ego — it lifts, spins (showing it every direction at once), and drops. For beginners: the final battle feels long and close; whirl precedes fall.
2. **Spear through the chest**: bhittvā śūlena vakṣasi — the tri-śūla (three-guṇa-transcending) through the chest (the “I”-seat). He falls shaking isles-oceans-mountains — the whole psycho-cosmos shudders as the central knot gives. Lifeless (gatāsuḥ): asmitā does not swoon (like Niśumbha) — it dies outright, for nothing stands behind the bare “I.”
3. **Cosmic settling**: portent-clouds calm, rivers resume courses (tathāsaṃs — “so they were,” order restored), world prasanna (clear), svāsthyam (health/establishment-in-Self — the word means both!), spotless sky. Then celebration total: joy-filled gods, gandharva song, apsarā dance, pure winds, fair sun, calm fires blazing quarter-echoing sounds. Every chapter ends dancing; the whole war ends in weather. Here ends Chapter 10 — Rudra-granthi pierced. Ch11-13: praise, fruit, boons (existing entries).`,
    keyPoints: [
      '**Lift, Whirl, Fling**: Grace lifts ego, spins it showing every direction, drops it — whirl precedes fall.',
      '**Chest-pierced “I” Dies Outright**: Asmitā does not swoon like mamatā — nothing stands behind it.',
      '**War Ends in Weather**: Settled clouds, resumed rivers, clear sky — order restored, celebration total.'
    ]
