    devanagari: 'देव्या हते तत्र महासुरेन्द्रे सेन्द्राः सुरा वह्निपुरोगमास्ताम् ।\nकात्यायनीं तुष्टुवुरिष्टलाभाद्विकाशिवक्त्रास्तु विकासिताशाः ॥१॥\nदेवि प्रपन्नार्तिहरे प्रसीद प्रसीद मातर्जगतोऽखिलस्य ।\nप्रसीद विश्वेश्वरि पाहि विश्वं त्वमीश्वरी देवि चराचरस्य ॥२॥\nआधारभूता जगतस्त्वमेका महीस्वरूपेण यतः स्थितासि ।\nअपां स्वरूपस्थितया त्वयैतदाप्याय्यते कृत्स्नमलङ्घयवीर्ये ॥३॥\nत्वं वैष्णवी शक्तिरनन्तवीर्या विश्वस्य बीजं परमासि माया ।\nसम्मोहितं देवि समस्तमेतत्त्वं वै प्रसन्ना भुवि मुक्तिहेतुः ॥४॥\nविद्याः समस्तास्तव देवि भेदाः स्त्रियः समस्ताः सकला जगत्सु ।\nत्वयैकया पूरितमम्बयैतत्का ते स्तुतिः स्तव्यपराऽपरोक्तिः ॥५॥\nसर्वभूता यदा देवी स्वर्गमुक्तिप्रदायिनी ।\nत्वं स्तुता स्तुतये का वा भवन्तु परमोक्तयः ॥६॥\nसर्वस्य बुद्धिरूपेण जनस्य हृदि संस्थिते ।\nस्वर्गापवर्गदे देवि नारायणि नमोऽस्तु ते ॥७॥\nकलाकाष्ठादिरूपेण परिणामप्रदायिनि ।\nविश्वस्योपरतौ शक्ते नारायणि नमोऽस्तु ते ॥८॥',
    iast: 'devyā hate tatra mahāsurendre sendrāḥ surā vahnipurogamāstām |\nkātyāyanīṃ tuṣṭuvuriṣṭalābhādvikāśivaktrāstu vikāsitāśāḥ ||1||\ndevi prapannārtihare prasīda prasīda mātarjagato\'khilasya |\nprasīda viśveśvari pāhi viśvaṃ tvamīśvarī devi carācarasya ||2||\nādhārabhūtā jagatastvamekā mahīsvarūpeṇa yataḥ sthitāsi |\napāṃ svarūpasthitayā tvayaitadāpyāyyate kṛtsnamalaṅghyavīrye ||3||\ntvaṃ vaiṣṇavī śaktiranantavīryā viśvasya bījaṃ paramāsi māyā |\nsammohitaṃ devi samastametattvaṃ vai prasannā bhuvi muktihetuḥ ||4||\nvidyāḥ samastāstava devi bhedāḥ striyaḥ samastāḥ sakalā jagatsu |\ntvayaikayā pūritamambayaitatkā te stutiḥ stavyaparā\'paroktiḥ ||5||\nsarvabhūtā yadā devī svargamuktipradāyinī |\ntvaṃ stutā stutaye kā vā bhavantu paramoktayaḥ ||6||\nsarvasya buddhirūpeṇa janasya hṛdi saṃsthite |\nsvargāpavargade devi nārāyaṇi namo\'stu te ||7||\nkalākāṣṭhādirūpeṇa pariṇāmapradāyini |\nviśvasyoparatau śakte nārāyaṇi namo\'stu te ||8||',
    conceptIds: ['narayani-stuti', 'cit-shakti', 'sadhana-samara'],
    translation: 'When the great demon-lord was slain by Devī, the gods with Indra, Fire at their head, praised Kātyāyanī for the wished gain — radiant-faced, their hopes blossomed: “Goddess, remover of the fallen suppliant’s pain, be gracious — be gracious, Mother of the whole world. Be gracious, Queen of the universe; guard the world — Thou art Īśvarī, Goddess of moving and unmoving. Sole ground of the world art Thou, standing in earth-form; by Thee abiding as the waters’ essence is all this nourished, O unsurpassable-valiant! Thou art Vaiṣṇavī power, endless-valiant, the world’s seed, supreme Māyā; by Thee, Goddess, is all this deluded — and pleased, Thou art on earth liberation’s cause. All lores are Thy distinctions, Goddess; all women in all worlds; by Thee alone, O Mother, is all this filled — what praise of Thee, transcending praise, can later words utter? When the Goddess, become all, grants heaven and liberation — what praise of praise can there be? Let highest words be! O Thou abiding in every man’s heart as intellect, giver of heaven and release — salutation to Thee, Nārāyaṇī! O giver of fruition in time’s measures, O potent one at the world’s withdrawal — salutation to Thee, Nārāyaṇī!”',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (Nārāyaṇī-stuti opens; victory assimilated):**
1. **Radiant-faced, hopes blossomed**: the gods praise “for the wished gain” (iṣṭalābhāt) — gratitude with content is honest, not mercenary. Victory’s stuti differs from crisis-stuti (Ch4-5): no plea, only beholding. For beginners: sing after wins, not only in need — thanksgiving completes the circuit.
2. **Prasīda thrice; ādhāra**: mercy thrice invoked (Mother, Queen, Īśvarī of moving-unmoving); then earth-support and water-nourishment — the Mother as infrastructure (addressed, wondrously, “O unsurpassable-valiant”!). Grace is not extra; it is the ground and the rain.
3. **Vaiṣṇavī-reprise**: verse 4 re-sings Brahmā’s 1.53-54 (dm-1-66) word for word — the delusion-liberation thesis, once heard from a teacher, is now the gods’ own realisation. Tradition reuses the crown verse: what liberated Brahmā liberates all. Then vidyāḥ/striyaḥ (all lores, all women = Her), sarvabhūtā (being-all), buddhi, kalā-kāṣṭhā (time-measures fruitioning). The Mothers-as-Nārāyaṇī follow (dm-11-12).`,
