    devanagari: 'सर्वमङ्गलमाङ्गल्ये शिवे सर्वार्थसाधिके।\nशरण्ये त्र्यम्बके गौरि नारायणि नमोऽस्तु ते॥\nसृष्टिस्थितिविनाशानां शक्तिभूते सनातनि।\nगुणाश्रये गुणमये नारायणि नमोऽस्तु ते॥\nशरणागतदीनार्तपरित्राणपरायणे।\nसर्वस्यार्तिहरे देवि नारायणि नमोऽस्तु ते॥',
    iast: 'sarvamaṅgalamāṅgalye śive sarvārthasādhike |\nśaraṇye tryambake gauri nārāyaṇi namo\'stu te ||\nsṛṣṭisthitivināśānāṃ śaktibhūte sanātani |\nguṇāśraye guṇamaye nārāyaṇi namo\'stu te ||\nśaraṇāgatadīnārtaparitrāṇaparāyaṇe |\nsarvasyārtihare devi nārāyaṇi namo\'stu te ||',
    conceptIds: ['narayani-stuti', 'cit-shakti'],
    translation: 'The gods sang the Nārāyaṇī Stuti: "O auspicious of all auspiciousness! O benevolent consort of Śiva! O fulfiller of all spiritual and material aims! O refuge of all, three-eyed Gauri, salutations to You, O Nārāyaṇī! You who are the eternal primal energy of creation, preservation, and dissolution, resting in the Guṇas yet embodying them—salutations to You! O redeemer of the afflicted who seek refuge, remover of all suffering, O Nārāyaṇī, salutations to You!"',
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary:**
Following the liberation of the three worlds from Śumbha and Niśumbha, the Devas pour their gratitude into the celebrated *Nārāyaṇī Stuti*. 
Notice She is addressed as *Nārāyaṇī*—the conscious active dynamic counterpart of *Nārāyaṇa* (the indwelling Self of all). She is *Sarvamaṅgala-māṅgalyā*: not only the bringer of good fortune, but the very essence of auspiciousness in all things auspicious. 
She is both *Guṇāśrayā* (the unattached transcendent substrate of the three Gunas) and *Guṇamayī* (the immanent manifest texture of nature). For the practitioner who has traversed the inner battlefield (*Sādhana-Samara*), this hymn represents the tranquil dawn of God-communion, where every breath is a hymn of praise and every creature is recognized as Her divine child.`,
